A Binary Tree is a tree data structure where each node has at most 2 children:
Left child
Right child

📌 Key Points:
Each node → max 2 children
Used in:
Binary Search Trees (BST)
Expression trees
Heaps
Structure is simple and flexible
Can become unbalanced (like a linked list 😅)

✅ Example:
      10
     /  \
    5    20
🔹 B-Tree
4

💡 What it is:
A B-Tree is a self-balancing multi-way tree used mainly in databases and file systems.

📌 Key Points:
Each node can have more than 2 children
Stores multiple keys in one node
Always balanced
Optimized for disk access (fewer reads)

✅ Example (Order 3):
      [10 | 20]
     /    |    \
   [5]  [15]  [25]
   
⚔️ Main Differences
Feature	Binary Tree 🌳	B-Tree �
Children per node	Max 2	Many (depends on order)
Keys per node	1	Multiple
Balance	Not always balanced	Always balanced
Usage	General purpose	Databases, file systems
Search speed	Slower if unbalanced	Fast (logarithmic)
Storage	Memory	Disk-friendly

⚡ Final One-Line Difference
👉 Binary Tree = 2 children per node
👉 B-Tree = many children + balanced + used for databases
